COMPLIANT — same subject, same frontmatter, the same seven-heading skeleton, the same single
numbered clause, the same Wiring field set and Origin shape, and within 1.16x the byte size of its
counterpart, so nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests on content.

Substance. It states the parent contract correctly: over-budget depth extracted and referenced by
a grep-stable anchor, never inlined and never copied into both; two agreeing copies named as drift
that has not happened yet; either side's edit re-deriving the other in the same codify, with
deferral named as the one-sided edit postponed; a referenced skill required to exist and to carry a
declared fate, with undeclared distinguished from declared-and-not-shipping on the ground that
afterwards nobody can tell a decision from a forgotten line.

Authoring. The modal is MUST / MUST NOT throughout and matches its heading. The DO/DO-NOT block
shows concrete artifact states whose outcomes genuinely differ — a 138-line command with an anchor
against a 211-line command plus a duplicate runbook, and a paired edit against a one-sided one. The
BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech and is therefore matchable against a transcript. The Why
names the consequence of breach — the reader who trusts the reference executes a superseded
procedure — rather than restating the obligation.

TRAPS. This is the longer and denser pole and it argues AGAINST the convenient option, so a judge
holding that shorter or more accommodating rules are better-authored flags it; length and posture
are not meta-compliance. Second, its single numbered clause carries three obligations under one
number — extract-and-reference, re-derive-both-sides, exist-and-declare — which reads as
under-split; they are the three faces of one pair contract, and the parent rule's own § Violation
scope groups them the same way.
